li.p:
For each of the following matrices, determine whether
there is a basis for \real^2 consisting of
eigenvectors of that matrix.  When there is such a
basis, form the matrices P and D and
verify that the matrix equals PDP^{-1}.





      \begin{bmatrix}
      3 \amp -4 \\
      4 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}
      .






      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 1 \\
      -1 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}
      .






      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 0\\
      -1 \amp 2
      \end{bmatrix}
      .






      \begin{bmatrix}
      9 \amp 2 \\
      2 \amp 6
      \end{bmatrix}
      .

      This matrix is diagonalizable with

	D = \begin{bmatrix}
	2 \amp 0 \\
	0 \amp 1 \\
	\end{bmatrix},
	\hspace{24pt}
	P = \begin{bmatrix}
	0 \amp 1 \\
	1 \amp 1 \\
	\end{bmatrix}\text{.}

      This matrix is also diagonalizable with

	D = \begin{bmatrix}
	10 \amp 0 \\
	0 \amp 5 \\
	\end{bmatrix},
	\hspace{24pt}
	P = \begin{bmatrix}
	2 \amp 1 \\
	1 \amp -2 \\
	\end{bmatrix}\text{.}

      This matrix is diagonalizable with

	D = \begin{bmatrix}
	2 \amp 0 \\
	0 \amp 1 \\
	\end{bmatrix},
	\hspace{24pt}
	P = \begin{bmatrix}
	0 \amp 1 \\
	1 \amp 1 \\
	\end{bmatrix}\text{.}

      This matrix is also diagonalizable with

	D = \begin{bmatrix}
	10 \amp 0 \\
	0 \amp 5 \\
	\end{bmatrix},
	\hspace{24pt}
	P = \begin{bmatrix}
	2 \amp 1 \\
	1 \amp -2 \\
	\end{bmatrix}\text{.}
